WEBVTT

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:40.930
comes now. Hello, Paul. Let me get a picture, Mr. Madwick. All right. Thank you. That's

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:44.490
all right. That's well. Hello, Paul. How are you? Fine. How are you? Who is that guy?

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:49.140
Paul Madwick. He's head of the voters' league. Hello, Mr. Madwick. Glad to see you. Hello,

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:54.020
son. He's the biggest crook in the state. I hear he feeds a thousand people a week. Is

00:01:54.020 --> 00:01:57.970
it true you're throwing over Governor Hartman? What about Ralph Henry and his reform party?

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:00.000
If Ralph Henry is so anxious to reform, he'll be so anxious to go.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.920
Why don't he start on that son of his?

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:03.830
He gets in more jams than the dead end, kids.

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:09.520
That's for talking about decent people.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.410
A little reform wouldn't do you any harm.

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.410
As a matter of fact, I think it would do the state good

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:16.270
if somebody would reform you, right out of existence.

00:02:16.270 --> 00:02:18.190
Get out of my way, you cheap crook.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:21.960
Hey, what a slugger. Who is that?

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.490
Ralph Henry's daughter.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.580
Yeah?

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:35.930
- Hey Ed, I just met the swellest dame.

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:37.930
- Yeah? - Yeah, she smacked me in the kisser.

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:41.200
- Hey Slosh, come here. - I don't care that.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:42.160
- What do you want? - Oh, never mind.

00:02:42.160 --> 00:02:44.310
Go out and tell old man Henry I want to see him, will you?

00:02:44.310 --> 00:02:46.440
- That's a good one. - What is?

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:48.860
- Are you serious? - Why not?

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:50.850
What'll Nick Vaughn and the rest of the boys say

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:52.850
if they hear you've been talking to a reform candidate?

00:02:52.850 --> 00:02:55.850
- Oh, what do I care what they say? - You better care.

00:02:55.850 --> 00:02:58.770
- Oh, yeah? - Yeah, if you want to keep on being, boss.

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:00.000
I better wash your mouth out.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.000
Get out, young fella.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Come here.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:17.200
Lay off there, will ya?

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:22.860
Matvick, I want to see him right away.

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:24.320
Yes, Mr. Varner.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:31.360
Porsche point, Ed.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:31.900
Come on, Ed.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.540
Ed, Nick Varna wants to see Paul.

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:40.980
Little Joe, brother, that's it.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:52.130
I'll tell him you're here.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:00.000
Yeah, take care of yourself.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:00.950
Right.

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:03.020
Hi, Ed.

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:08.290
Nick Farner's downstairs.

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:10.810
Yeah?

00:04:10.810 --> 00:04:12.860
He wants to see you right away, Paul.

00:04:12.860 --> 00:04:15.410
Now tell him I'm busy.

00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:16.360
But Paul...

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:17.360
Tell him if he wants to wait around.

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:19.360
Maybe I can find some time for him later.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:20.380
Maybe.

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:22.880
Tell him.

00:04:31.210 --> 00:04:34.440
You really going through with that crazy idea?

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:35.440
What crazy idea?

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.490
Backing old man Henry and the reform ticket.

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:38.410
That's right.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:39.410
That's wrong.

00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.400
That's wrong?

00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:43.380
Yeah, as wrong as those socks.

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:46.230
Wait a minute, what's wrong with them?

00:04:46.230 --> 00:04:47.940
The clock.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:48.770
Yeah?

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:49.680
It ticks too loud.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:52.560
You know, I thought they was pretty.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:59.160
Yeah, old man Henry and me is cooking up the deal tonight at dinner at his house.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.900
Say it, it's his daughter's birthday.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:03.020
What do you think I ought to give her?

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:04.570
I'll make a good impression.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:05.130
Sure.

00:05:05.490 --> 00:05:05.730
Nothing.

00:05:06.490 --> 00:05:07.110
But why?

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:08.830
Because you're not supposed to give people things

00:05:08.830 --> 00:05:10.330
unless you're sure they'd like to get them from you.

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:12.490
Oh, well, maybe.

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:15.730
But I'm not going to pass up an opportunity like this.

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:16.390
You see, I like...

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:19.240
All right, give her a convertible or a handful of diamonds.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:22.900
Well, maybe you know more about it than I do, I guess.

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:25.790
Think you'll play ball after election?

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.430
I know he will.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:29.700
Ha, ha.

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:32.800
- Why, he's practically giving me the key to his house.

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:35.730
- Yeah, glass key.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:37.310
Be careful it doesn't break off in your hand.

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:42.140
- Don't worry, it won't.

00:05:42.140 --> 00:05:51.040
- How far has that doll gotta hooks into you?

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:54.570
- I'm gonna marry Janet Henry.

00:05:54.570 --> 00:05:57.900
Of course, nobody knows about it yet except you and me.

00:05:57.900 --> 00:05:59.110
- Well, you better insist on the wedding

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:00.000
before election day.

00:06:00.990 --> 00:06:03.890
- Then you can be sure you're a pound of flesh.

00:06:03.890 --> 00:06:07.390
- But Mr. Barna, he left positive orders to...

00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:09.220
- Shut up, Oswald.

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:32.710
Come on in.

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:36.320
Have a chair.

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:37.980
A cigar.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:43.730
Thanks.

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:46.000
You know, Paul, they closed my places last night.

00:06:46.000 --> 00:06:48.230
Broke a lot of equipment.

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:49.830
Yeah.

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:50.830
We read about it in the paper.

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:54.190
Rainey said the order came straight from you.

00:06:54.190 --> 00:06:57.440
Well, what do you think of that, Ed?

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:00.000
I think somebody ought to give Rainey a nice long time.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.590
long vacation. Business is business and politics is politics. But they ought to be kept apart.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:13.730
I bought protection, Paul, and I want it. Well, I tell you, Nick, we're going to clean up this town.

00:07:15.380 --> 00:07:21.850
I'm too big to take the boot from you now. You may be too big to take it laying down, Nick,

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:28.720
but you're going to take it. You are taking it. Oh, no, I'm not. The Golden Club's open tonight.

00:07:34.410 --> 00:07:35.010
Get me Rainy.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:38.520
Oh, Rainy, it's Paul.

00:07:38.740 --> 00:07:39.240
How are the folks?

00:07:41.080 --> 00:07:42.700
Hey, uh, look, Rainy.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:47.650
I just got the tip that Nick is opening his golden clubs tonight.

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:51.980
Yeah, slam them down so hard they'll splash.

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:58.290
Now you know where you stand.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:23.640
tell you you haven't got a thing to worry about we mustn't forget the opposition what that guy he

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:29.490
reminds me of an electric fan buzzing around all day nothing comes out but wind you know politics is

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:30.000
simple

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:31.740
All you need is a little muscle.

00:08:31.740 --> 00:08:34.150
But muscle gave me my start in politics.

00:08:34.150 --> 00:08:37.670
It was when I was assistant to old man Zimmerman, the plumber.

00:08:37.670 --> 00:08:39.780
Mr. Madvig, you're joking!

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:41.560
You mean about the plumbing or the muscle?

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:43.620
Well, I just can't imagine you as a plumber.

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:44.420
No.

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:46.580
I'll drop up to your bathroom someday and show you.

00:08:53.020 --> 00:08:56.410
Well, Paul, I must say you've come a long way since then.

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:57.170
Yeah.

00:08:57.170 --> 00:09:00.000
Coffee will be served in the living room, Mr. Madvig.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:09.140
He's funny, but don't you think he'll get a bit tiresome as a steady boarder?

00:09:09.140 --> 00:09:11.890
Paul's support means the governorship for me.

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:14.000
I know, but is being governor worth it?

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:15.000
Don't joke.

00:09:15.000 --> 00:09:18.560
And you must be nice to him, Janet, anyway until after the election.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:22.180
All right, Dad, at least there'll be some laughs.

00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:31.140
Why weren't you dinner?

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:32.980
With Matvick, I'd sooner cut my throat.

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:34.540
Look, I've got to have some money.

00:09:34.680 --> 00:09:36.600
But I've already given you every penny I have.

00:09:36.660 --> 00:09:37.880
Well, then ask Dad for a long time.

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:38.740
I can't.

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:41.450
Oh, Taylor, why don't you stop gambling?

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:43.250
Don't go sanctimonious on me.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:46.310
But if you'd only stop for a while...

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:50.300
Taylor, Janet, do you mind, please?

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:57.580
Taylor, do you realize that your attitude toward Matvick is very embarrassing to me?

00:09:57.840 --> 00:09:58.830
I'm not running for governor.

00:09:58.990 --> 00:09:59.730
Well, I am.

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:02.910
Your gambling, your night clubbing, and your drunkenness has got to stop.

00:10:03.340 --> 00:10:04.470
That's just talking.

00:10:04.510 --> 00:10:05.010
I mean it.

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:06.750
On Monday, I'm going to find you a job.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:08.530
If that doesn't straighten you out, I'll...

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:08.930
Where are you going?

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:09.600
Out.

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:22.690
Hey, there's that bullmine.

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:23.930
Come on.

00:10:24.130 --> 00:10:28.220
Come on in, Ed.

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:31.810
I'd like you to meet Janet Henry.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:33.060
This is Ed Beaumont.

00:10:35.130 --> 00:10:35.610
Howdy, too.

00:10:36.980 --> 00:10:39.040
So you're Mr. Medvick's assistant.

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:41.700
He's more of a partner, I'd say.

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:43.110
Anything to eat, Ed?

00:10:44.100 --> 00:10:46.830
Oh, Mr. Henry, do you know Ed Beaumont?

00:10:47.320 --> 00:10:48.820
Oh, yes. Yes, I know who you are.

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:50.860
Oh, here are those figures you wanted for Mr. Henry.

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:51.860
Oh, yeah, thanks, Ed.

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:54.460
Won't you stay for coffee, Mr. Beaumont?

00:10:54.740 --> 00:10:55.540
Yeah, sure. Come on.

00:10:56.520 --> 00:10:58.200
A little drop of jam will wake you up.

00:11:02.880 --> 00:11:10.360
some coffee for Mr. Beaumont. And brandy? Thank you. You know, Ed, I was just telling

00:11:10.360 --> 00:11:14.390
Janet, Miss Henry here, about the time I was in the newspaper business. Yes, you were

00:11:14.390 --> 00:11:18.760
saying you had a job at The Observer, but you didn't say what it was. Oh, well, it was

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:22.900
sort of like in the circulation department, you know? You see, I used to go around to

00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:27.040
different newsstands and I'd ask for a paper. If the guy had hand me The Observer, that

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:30.000
was okay. If he'd hand me The Post, I'd slug him. That was a teaching

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:35.830
him a lesson, you know. Oh, you were a sort of an observer missionary. Oh, no, because a little while

00:11:35.830 --> 00:11:40.370
later I made the same deal with the post. Well, how could you serve both newspapers, Paul? You see,

00:11:40.670 --> 00:11:45.390
if the guy handed me the observer, I'd slug him for the post. If he hands me the post, I'd slug him

00:11:45.390 --> 00:11:51.070
for the observer. It was very simple. You certainly were a two-fisted newspaperman, Mr. Madvick.

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:56.180
Wasn't he, Mr. Boma? Yeah, but there was just one hitch. I used to have to be very careful about

00:11:56.180 --> 00:11:57.840
repeating. But once I missed.

00:11:58.250 --> 00:12:00.000
I remember it was on 3rd and

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.920
Broadway, I slugged the guy for handing me the observer.

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:06.530
About a week later, I got balled up and I found myself in the same spot.

00:12:06.990 --> 00:12:09.530
Well, the guy hands me the post, so I have to slug him again.

00:12:09.630 --> 00:12:12.030
You should have seen the expression on that fellow's face.

00:12:12.730 --> 00:12:15.030
There was enough there for an expression.

00:12:15.170 --> 00:12:17.770
Oh, sure. I just used to slug him easy with the left.

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:19.190
But you haven't heard the topper.

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:23.640
Quite a while later, when I'd gotten out of the racket, I found myself back in the same spot.

00:12:24.080 --> 00:12:26.480
Well, the guy saw me coming, and what do you think he handed me?

00:12:26.710 --> 00:12:28.670
The Christian Science Monitor.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.610
- And you slugged him again?

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:35.290
- No, I didn't have no deal with the monitor.

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:38.140
- What a pity.

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:39.720
- I must be going.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:41.270
- Oh, good night, Mr. Henry.

00:12:41.270 --> 00:12:42.260
- Good night.

00:12:42.260 --> 00:12:43.100
- Good night, Paul.

00:12:43.100 --> 00:12:43.920
See you in the morning, Ed.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:52.730
- I'll see you to the door, Mr. Beaumont.

00:12:52.730 --> 00:12:54.630
Sorry you're leaving so soon, Mr. Beaumont.

00:12:54.630 --> 00:12:57.080
I'm sure you have some interesting stories, too.

00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:03.530
I think you've had enough laughs from the long side of the tracks for one evening.

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:07.610
Well, hardly the type people laugh at.

00:13:08.340 --> 00:13:11.130
There are people who don't laugh at Paul.

00:13:11.610 --> 00:13:13.670
Well, he is amusing for a limited time.

00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:16.610
To be with him every day, how can you stand it?

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:19.990
I get along very well with Paul because he's on the dead up and up.

00:13:20.990 --> 00:13:22.070
Why don't you try it sometime?

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:41.270
Ed, I've been looking all over for you.

00:13:41.430 --> 00:13:43.250
I've been slumming with your brother up at the Henry house.

00:13:43.670 --> 00:13:45.880
Ed, I need your help. I've got to have some money.

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:48.610
Yeah? How much?

00:13:49.000 --> 00:13:49.840
All you've got.

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:54.230
Don't ask any questions, Ed.

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:56.700
Please, don't even tell Paul.

00:13:58.090 --> 00:13:58.650
All right, Steve.

00:13:58.650 --> 00:13:58.870
All right, Steve.

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:02.880
Your sweetheart.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:42.470
Taylor Henry. Hello, Nick. I've got 500 for you. But Nick will have the rest in a couple of days, I promise.

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:51.720
He's sore, but he'll take the 500.

00:15:00.220 --> 00:15:18.710
- How can I ever thank you, baby?

00:15:18.710 --> 00:15:19.590
- Let's go, Snip.

00:15:33.040 --> 00:15:33.460
Put him on.

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:35.640
Who do you think you are, Beaumont?

00:15:36.650 --> 00:15:37.050
Hurry up.

00:15:37.980 --> 00:15:39.730
I'm not going. You get out of here.

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:41.380
Share with you.

00:15:41.480 --> 00:15:42.300
Taylor, stop him.

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:43.900
Oh!

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:05.340
Here's your key, Snip.

00:16:05.640 --> 00:16:08.440
I hate you.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:09.520
You'll outlive it, baby.

00:16:09.700 --> 00:16:10.640
No, I won't ever.

00:16:10.780 --> 00:16:11.220
Good night.

00:16:11.800 --> 00:16:12.850
I'm coming in.

00:16:13.660 --> 00:16:16.440
Lock me in my room, I suppose.

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:17.720
Not a bad idea.

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:20.080
You can't stop me from seeing Taylor.

00:16:20.520 --> 00:16:21.600
Neither you nor Paul.

00:16:22.050 --> 00:16:24.220
I've got a right to happiness just like anybody else.

00:16:24.590 --> 00:16:25.710
I'm free, white...

00:16:25.710 --> 00:16:26.270
And 18.

00:16:26.530 --> 00:16:27.770
What's wrong with being 18?

00:16:28.330 --> 00:16:30.000
Lots of girls get married when they're 18.

00:16:30.480 --> 00:16:32.120
My mother, even.

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:34.520
Hey, what are you two barking about this time?

00:16:35.120 --> 00:16:36.520
What's the matter? Didn't they feed you at the party?

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:40.400
Yeah. Did I hear something about Taylor Henry?

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:43.080
Have you been seeing him again?

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:44.700
No, I just happened to mention his name.

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:47.060
You don't have to lie for me. Yes, I saw him tonight.

00:16:47.300 --> 00:16:50.860
After all I've said, where were they?

00:16:51.440 --> 00:16:52.060
In a nightclub.

00:16:52.540 --> 00:16:54.470
We weren't. I was at his apartment.

00:16:55.580 --> 00:16:55.960
You were?

00:16:56.080 --> 00:16:57.830
And I don't see what business it is of yours.

00:16:58.150 --> 00:17:00.000
You're supposed to be my brother, not a watchdog.

00:17:02.400 --> 00:17:06.660
No sister of mine is going to be taken in by any cheap, chiseling, tin-horned playboy like...

00:17:06.660 --> 00:17:08.840
He's not a playboy. You know it.

00:17:08.990 --> 00:17:10.510
Is this the first time you've been to his apartment?

00:17:10.820 --> 00:17:12.660
No, it isn't. I've been there dozens of times.

00:17:32.750 --> 00:17:34.550
Paul's gone up to Mr. Henry's house after Taylor.

00:17:35.030 --> 00:17:36.000
He's gonna kill him.

00:17:36.100 --> 00:17:36.860
Take it easy, Snip.

00:17:36.940 --> 00:17:37.840
Paul's not that crazy.

00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:40.810
I'd be a chump to go up there at this time of night.

00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:42.360
Now you go to sleep.

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:30.000
- I thought you went home. - Did you find Taylor Henry?

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:41.620
- Opal phoned you, huh? - Yeah. Did you find him?

00:18:43.760 --> 00:18:48.280
I found the old man. Had a long talk with him.

00:18:49.060 --> 00:18:53.860
- Wasn't Taylor there? - No. Why?

00:18:54.760 --> 00:18:58.320
He's lying outside the house in the gutter. He's dead.

00:19:04.000 --> 00:19:06.400
- So?

00:19:06.400 --> 00:19:12.820
- Didn't you understand what I said?

00:19:12.820 --> 00:19:18.750
He was killed.

00:19:18.750 --> 00:19:24.780
- What do you want me to do about it?

